{
 "_note": [
  "The decoration catalogue, by hand. One entry per find.",
  "",
  "Read against art_source/Decoration_Clean_Dirty, which holds two layer groups: 'Decoration'",
  "(the restored piece, as the shed shows it) and 'Decoration Dirty' (the same piece as the",
  "lake shows it, one sprite and no views). tools/build_decor.py turns this file plus those",
  "layers into assets/decor_clean.png, assets/decor_dirty.png and the decor half of",
  "assets/pieces.json.",
  "",
  "Why by hand: a set is several sprites packed into ONE layer, side by side or stacked, and",
  "nothing in the pixels says whether the second sprite is the same chair turned sideways, a",
  "second style of the same thing, or the same fridge with its door open. Those three mean",
  "three different mechanics. Gap detection can find the rectangles; only a person can say",
  "what they are. The rectangles here were read off the layers' own pixel islands, so they",
  "are the art's numbers rather than guesses, but the roles are authored.",
  "",
  "kind:",
  "  SINGLE  - one view. Nothing to cycle.",
  "  ROTATE  - the same piece from several sides. R cycles it while carrying.",
  "  VARIANT - several styles of the same thing. R picks one. Not a rotation.",
  "  STATE   - the same piece doing two things. E toggles it once it is standing.",
  "",
  "mirror: append a horizontally flipped copy of the 'side' view to the cycle, so a room is",
  "not full of chairs all facing the same way. Only the three-view sets get it: a two-view",
  "set is front and side as drawn, and that is the whole cycle.",
  "",
  "views[].rect is [x, y, w, h] inside that layer's own trimmed PNG. Omit it to mean the",
  "whole layer.",
  "",
  "Every entry needs a 'dirty' layer or it cannot be netted, and netting is the only way into",
  "the shed. Both pet beds share one, which is the point of VARIANT: one find, two ways to",
  "stand it.",
  "",
  "copies: how many of this find are hidden in the lake, and so how many the shed can hold",
  "at once. One unless said otherwise. The chairs are four each: a dining table with a",
  "single chair at it is not a room anybody lives in, and four of them turned four ways is",
  "what the rotation is for. Every copy is the same find with the same one dirty sprite —",
  "they are netted separately and stood separately, not one object that multiplies.",
  "",
  "place: where the piece goes. 'floor' (default) stands on the boards; 'wall' hangs on the",
  "back wall and takes no floor cell (the paintings); 'small' stands on the floor but may be",
  "set over a big piece and is drawn just after whatever it sits on (pots, lamp, clock, toy,",
  "globe). Richard, 2026-09-13.",
  "",
  "base: how many of the picture's bottom rows of cells stand on the floor, per view, in the",
  "views' own order. The rest of the picture rises up the back wall when the piece is pushed",
  "against it. The art is a three-quarter view, so a front-on sofa's base is its seat depth",
  "and a side-on one's is nearly the whole picture. One number per face, and a mirrored set",
  "counts as four. Rugs and anything lying flat default to their whole height, everything",
  "else to one cell.",
  "",
  "base_px: the same thing in the drawing's own PIXELS, for a piece a whole cell is too",
  "coarse for. A cell is eight pixels, so the least a base can say in cells is eight, and on",
  "a 23-pixel pot that is a third of the picture held off the back wall with nothing drawn in",
  "the gap (Richard, 2026-09-19, issue #30). Never authored alongside base - the builder",
  "refuses both. Only the two pots have one; a base is also the walker block, the host probe",
  "and the band a walker sorts over, so two pixels is right for a pot standing on its foot",
  "ring and would be wrong for a sofa.",
  "",
  "seat: where a dog's feet go on this view, in drawn pixels up from the picture's bottom",
  "edge; 0 on a view nothing may lie on (2026-09-19, issue #30). Authoring one is the whole",
  "rule - there is no test of a view's role, because decor_bed's views are colours and",
  "decor_pet_bed's are shapes, and a gate on the name 'front' would give both beds no seat",
  "at all. The sofa and the loveseat get one on their front face only: seen from the side or",
  "the back, a dog laid on the cushion is cropped by the backrest.",
  "",
  "Both are measured against the DRAWN picture, so they already carry scale (the bed is 1.5).",
  "Measured off assets/decor_clean.png, not off tools/last_decor_views.png, which is",
  "git-ignored and which nothing in the repo writes.",
  "",
  "dirty_piece: for a find born from the rubbish — its lake sprite is that rubbish kind's",
  "own picture, cut off the lake sheet, and the rubbish kind stays in the fill as well. A",
  "clean view whose layer PNG is missing falls back to that same picture until it is painted.",
  "",
  "scale: how many times bigger than painted the shed draws a piece (default 1). The bed is",
  "1.5 (Richard, 2026-09-13: 'a bit bigger') - a whole screen pixel per painted one at the",
  "room's usual zoom of 2, where 1.25 would be two and a half. The lake never sees it.",
  "The stove and the kitchen counter are 0.75 (Richard, same day: 'decrease size').",
  "",
  "Two ways in, since 2026-09-20. A piece drawn as a GROUP under 'Decoration' in the PSD,",
  "one layer per view, with a matching layer under 'Decoration Dirty', needs no entry here",
  "at all: tools/build_decor.py reads it off the PSD's own layer tree, and the group name's",
  "(rotate|variant|state) suffix is what says the mechanic. The 'entries' below are the 37",
  "finds authored before that, several views to a layer and split by hand-read rectangles.",
  "A piece may be in one place or the other, never both.",
  "",
  "'tuning' is where the numbers live for a piece that came in off the PSD, keyed by its",
  "name (decor_<title>): place, base, base_px, seat, scale, copies, mirror, dirty_piece.",
  "The numbers never go in the PSD - they are set by eye against the shed and retuned",
  "without reopening the art.",
  "",
  "face / state (2026-09-20): a view may say which way it faces and whether it is switched on.",
  "R moves to the next face (keeping the state when that drawing exists), E flips the state",
  "keeping the face, and does nothing where the other drawing was never made - the kitchen",
  "counter's side. Left out, they follow the kind: ROTATE/VARIANT count faces, STATE counts",
  "states. View 0 is what a piece comes out of the store as, so it is face 0, switched off.",
  "",
  "light: what a piece gives off while switched on - fire (warm pool and the crackle), warm",
  "(warm pool, silent: the lamps), cold (the open fridge). Anything else switches in silence."
 ],
